WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.280
Today, we're diving into the&nbsp;
world of web authentication.

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:05.800
We’ll explore the two most common approaches:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:10.040
session-based authentication&nbsp;
and JSON Web Tokens, or JWTs.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:14.160
We’ll walk through the flow of each&nbsp;
mechanism and discuss their pros and cons.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.720
By the end, you'll have a clear&nbsp;
understanding of when to use each one.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.200
Let's get started!

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.680
First, let's walk through the flow&nbsp;
of session-based authentication.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.560
The user sends their login&nbsp;
credentials to the server.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.760
The server verifies these credentials.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.360
If they're valid, it creates a new session.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:35.680
The server then stores the session data, typically&nbsp;
in a database or in-memory cache like Redis.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:40.560
This data might include the user ID,&nbsp;
session expiration time, and other metadata.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:45.960
The server sends back a response with a unique&nbsp;
session ID, usually in the form of a cookie.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:51.440
On subsequent requests, the client automatically&nbsp;
sends the session ID cookie with each request.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.600
The server takes the session ID, looks&nbsp;
up the corresponding session data in its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:00.320
session store, and uses that data to&nbsp;
authenticate and process the request.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.440
The key point is that with&nbsp;
session-based authentication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.400
the server is responsible for&nbsp;
creating and storing the session data.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:12.640
It then uses the session ID as a key to&nbsp;
retrieve this data on future requests.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:16.640
One advantage of sessions is that&nbsp;
revoking a session is straightforward.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:19.000
Since the session data is stored on the server,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.400
the server can simply delete or&nbsp;
invalidate the session at any time.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:28.080
However, in a distributed system where&nbsp;
your application runs on multiple servers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.240
all those servers need access&nbsp;
to the same session data.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:35.080
This is typically achieved by using&nbsp;
a centralized session store that all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:39.120
servers can access, like Redis&nbsp;
or a distributed SQL database.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.320
While this works well, it does add some&nbsp;
complexity and potential latency to each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:48.040
request, as the server needs to make&nbsp;
a separate trip to the session store.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.160
Now, let's look at the flow&nbsp;
of JWT-based authentication.

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:54.400
First, the user sends their&nbsp;
login credentials to the server.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.560
The server verifies these credentials.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:59.160
If they're valid, it generates a JWT.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.840
The server signs the JWT with a secret key.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.160
This signature ensures the integrity&nbsp;
of the token, preventing tampering.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:10.920
The server then sends back the JWT to the&nbsp;
client, typically in the response body.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:14.880
The client stores the JWT, usually&nbsp;
in local storage or a cookie.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.280
On subsequent requests, the client&nbsp;
sends the JWT in the request headers.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.440
The server verifies the JWT signature.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.680
If it's valid, the server&nbsp;
trusts the data in the token&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:28.000
and uses it for authentication and authorization.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:33.000
The critical difference here is that with JWTs,&nbsp;
the server doesn't store any session state.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:38.040
All the necessary data is contained within the&nbsp;
token itself, which is stored on the client.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:40.320
This makes JWTs stateless.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:43.480
For signing JWTs, there are&nbsp;
several algorithms available,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:47.560
with HMAC, RSA , and ECDSA being the most common.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:50.160
HMAC is a symmetric signing method,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.400
which means the same secret key is&nbsp;
used to sign and verify the token.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.280
This is simpler and more efficient, but&nbsp;
it requires sharing the secret key with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:02.880
any service that needs to verify the&nbsp;
token, which can be a security concern.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:07.480
RSA and ECDSA, on the other hand,&nbsp;
are asymmetric signing methods.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:11.640
They use a private key to sign the&nbsp;
token and a public key to verify it.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:16.400
This allows for a more secure architecture&nbsp;
where the private key is kept secret and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:21.600
only used for signing, while any service&nbsp;
can verify the token using the public key.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:26.760
However, this adds some complexity and&nbsp;
computational overhead compared to HMAC.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:32.120
The choice of signing algorithm depends on your&nbsp;
security requirements and system architecture.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:36.320
If you have a monolithic application or&nbsp;
trust all the services in your system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:38.360
HMAC might be sufficient.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:42.000
But if you have a microservice&nbsp;
architecture or need to share JWTs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:49.000
with untrusted third-party services, RSA&nbsp;
or ECDSA provide a more secure solution.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.640
One challenge with JWTs is&nbsp;
handling token expiration.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:56.080
If a token is stolen, it can&nbsp;
be used until it expires.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:01.800
To mitigate this, you can use refresh tokens&nbsp;
in combination with short-lived access tokens.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:05.200
The access token is the actual JWT&nbsp;
used for authentication on each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:10.120
request. It has a short expiration&nbsp;
time, typically around 15 minutes.

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:12.040
The refresh token, on the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:16.280
has a much longer expiration time,&nbsp;
perhaps several days or weeks.

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:20.720
When the access token expires, instead&nbsp;
of requiring the user to log in again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:25.320
the client can send the refresh token to&nbsp;
a special token endpoint on the server.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:28.600
The server checks if the refresh&nbsp;
token is valid and hasn't been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:33.120
revoked. If everything checks out,&nbsp;
the server issues a new access token.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:38.200
This process happens behind the scenes,&nbsp;
without requiring interaction from the user.

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:41.840
This approach strikes a balance&nbsp;
between security and user experience.

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:47.440
The short-lived access tokens limit the window&nbsp;
of potential misuse if a token is stolen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:51.800
while the long-lived refresh tokens&nbsp;
allow users to remain authenticated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:55.920
for an extended period without&nbsp;
needing to log in repeatedly.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:59.840
It's important to note that the refresh&nbsp;
token is only sent when the access&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:02.800
token has expired, not on every request.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:06.880
The access token is sent on every&nbsp;
request that requires authentication.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:11.880
So, when should you use session-based&nbsp;
authentication, and when are JWTs a better choice?

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:17.120
Session-based authentication is a good fit when&nbsp;
you need the ability to revoke sessions instantly.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.800
If a user reports their account as compromised,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:23.440
you can immediately invalidate&nbsp;
their session on the server side.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:28.400
Sessions are also a good choice if you already&nbsp;
have a centralized data store for other purposes.

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:33.560
In this case, you can leverage that existing&nbsp;
infrastructure for session storage as well.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:37.600
However, it's important to keep in mind&nbsp;
that using a centralized session store does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:43.200
add some latency to each request, as the server&nbsp;
needs to fetch the session data from the store.

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:48.200
Finally, sessions keep sensitive data on the&nbsp;
server, which can be a security advantage.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:52.720
On the other hand, JWTs are a great choice&nbsp;
when you need a stateless architecture.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:56.200
Because JWTs store all the&nbsp;
necessary data in the token itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:00.480
your server doesn't need to keep track&nbsp;
of sessions in memory or in a database.

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:05.560
This makes it much easier to scale your&nbsp;
application horizontally across multiple servers.

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:10.360
JWTs are also useful when you need to share&nbsp;
authentication data with other services.

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:13.040
For instance, in a microservice architecture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:17.480
a JWT generated by the authentication&nbsp;
service can be verified and trusted by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:22.640
other services without needing to contact&nbsp;
the authentication service on each request.

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:24.120
If you do choose JWTs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:28.720
consider implementing refresh tokens to&nbsp;
balance security and user experience.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:34.240
Refresh tokens allow you to use short-lived access&nbsp;
tokens to limit the window of potential misuse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:38.760
while still allowing users to remain&nbsp;
authenticated for an extended period.

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:44.640
Ultimately, the choice depends on the specific&nbsp;
needs and architecture of your application.

